  message: "CONFIRMED from the other side, 2026-09-10, incidental to WI-kigub (PR forthcoming).\n\nThis item's `:httpc.request` limb is real and I hit it head-on while deciding how\nthe elixir analyzer should spell an atom module in the module slot.\n\nExecuted rather than reasoned (`_module_matches`, live):\n    catalog='ets'     hint=':ets'    -> False\n    catalog='ets'     hint='ets'     -> True\n    catalog=':httpc'  hint='httpc'   -> False\n    catalog='httpc'   hint=':httpc'  -> False\n\nOf the elixir catalogue's 60 distinct modules (parent-merged with erlang via\n_CATALOG_PARENTS), `:httpc` is the ONLY one spelled with a leading colon. Every\nother OTP module arrives from the erlang parent spelled bare — `ets`, `file`,\n`gen_tcp` — which is also what the erlang analyzer emits\n(`erlang:ets:0-0:insert:function`). So the colon-spelled row is the odd one out\nin its own catalogue, in both directions.\n\nWHY IT CHANGED NO VERDICT FOR ME, which is the useful half: both rows carry\n`boundary: net_send`, so the duplicate inflates the denominator exactly as this\nitem says but classifies identically. WI-kigub emits the module WITHOUT the\ncolon, which matches erlang's `httpc` row and misses elixir's `:httpc` one; the\n`host-secret-no-network` verdict it moved is unaffected either way. That is why\nI did NOT fold the fix into WI-kigub — it would have been scope expansion with\nno behavioural difference on the repository I was measuring.\n\nFor whoever takes this: deleting the `:httpc` row (not re-spelling it) looks\nright, since the erlang parent already supplies `httpc.request` with the same\nboundary and the same notes-level meaning. I did not verify that against the\ncpp limb."  # 3d4e
